//? Title: Cuthill–McKee: Queue Order, Bandwidth and the Effect of Reversal
//? Subtitle: A hand-checkable path, the original forty-row pattern, a counterexample and measured traversal work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A simultaneous row and column permutation changes where a sparse symmetric
matrix stores its nonzeros without changing the underlying linear system.
Cuthill–McKee (CM) uses a breadth-first queue with low-degree choices to seek
a narrow band. We begin with five vertices, retain the original forty-row
shuffled band example, and distinguish bandwidth from lower profile and
Cholesky fill. Reversing any ordering preserves bandwidth exactly; its value
is a possible improvement in envelope shape. A five-vertex star shows that
CM can even worsen an already good bandwidth. Native datamunge APIs provide
complete actual traversal traces, checked graph normalization, permutations,
profile diagnostics and structural coordinates. Counts and exact small-graph
controls support the claims; no solver speedup or optimal ordering is assumed.
)

\toc

> Five vertices: see the queue before the matrix

Take the path 0—3—1—4—2. Its original labels scatter edges three positions
from the diagonal. Degrees are (1,2,1,2,2), so the smallest (degree,id) root
is 0. Removing 0 discovers 3; removing 3 discovers 1; the queue continues
through 4 to 2. The forward order is (0,3,1,4,2). Each edge then spans one
position. All of this comes from the actual traversal, including parents,
levels and discoveries, rather than from an independently reconstructed walk.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cuthill_queue.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> graph{{3},{3,4},{4},{0,1},{1,2}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace(graph);
  const auto before=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,{0,1,2,3,4});
  const auto after=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,t.order);
  if(t.order!=std::vector<int>{0,3,1,4,2} || before.bandwidth!=3 || after.bandwidth!=1 || t.adjacency_scans!=8) return 1;
  std::print("| removal position | original vertex | degree | parent | BFS level | newly enqueued |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=1.6;node.height=1.2;node.circle=true;node.font_size=18;node.fill={219,234,254};
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<t.steps.size();++k) {
    const auto& s=t.steps[k];const int u=s.vertex;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,u,t.degree[u],t.parent[u],t.level[u],s.newly_enqueued);
    d.node(std::to_string(u),{2.5*k,0},std::format("vertex {}",u),node)
     .text({2.5*k,-1.4},std::format("position {}",k),18)
     .text({2.5*k,-2},std::format("level {}",t.level[u]),17);
    if(t.parent[u]!=u) d.edge(std::to_string(t.parent[u]),std::to_string(u),"discover");
  }
  d.text({5,2},"CM follows the path: each original edge now spans one position",21)
   .text({5,-3},std::format("bandwidth {} to {}; {} directed entries scanned; {} non-root enqueues",before.bandwidth,after.bandwidth,t.adjacency_scans,t.neighbor_enqueues),19)
   .save_svg("build/cuthill_queue.svg");
  std::print("\nForward order {}; inverse positions {}.\n",t.order,t.position);
}
```
