\caption(The path drawn in the computed order. Arrows show actual discovery parents; labels retain original ids. The root parents itself and is not a non-root enqueue.)
\alttext(Five circles show original vertices 0, 3, 1, 4 and 2 in a line. Their new positions and BFS levels run from zero to four.)

> Specify the ordering and derive what it changes

Let p[k] be the original vertex placed at new position k, and let q be its
inverse: q[p[k]]=k. For P[k,p[k]]=1, the permuted system is
B=PAPᵀ, y=Px and c=Pb. Solving By=c and returning x=Pᵀy solves the original
Ax=b. The permutation preserves symmetry and positive definiteness when
present; a graph pattern alone does not imply either numerical property
beyond structural symmetry. For any nonzero z, zᵀBz=(Pᵀz)ᵀA(Pᵀz)>0
when A is SPD, since Pᵀz is nonzero.

The off-diagonal structural graph has an edge {u,v} for each symmetric
nonzero pair. This report assumes diagonal positions exist for storage
illustrations. Define the half-bandwidth and row-envelope profiles by

\[
\beta(p)=\max_{\{u,v\}\in E}|q[u]-q[v]|,\quad
\ell_i=i-\min(\{i\}\cup N_i),\quad
u_i=\max(\{i\}\cup N_i)-i,\quad
L(p)=\sum_i\ell_i,\quad U(p)=\sum_i u_i.
\]
\alttext(Half-bandwidth is the maximum distance across an edge. Each row has lower and upper envelope widths measured from its diagonal to its first or last structural entry. Profiles sum these widths.)

Here N_i contains neighbouring positions in the permuted graph; an edgeless
graph has β=L=U=0. We call β simply bandwidth below; a symmetric full band
has width at most 2β+1, including the diagonal. A lower skyline allocates
n+L slots, including diagonal and any holes inside the envelope. That is not
the number of actual nonzeros in the matrix or its Cholesky factor.

Datamunge implements a fixed, deterministic CM variant:

1. Normalize each adjacency row, ignoring self-loops and repeated entries;
   reject invalid endpoints or any missing reverse edge.
2. Among unvisited vertices, start the next component at the smallest pair
   (distinct off-diagonal degree, original id).
3. Remove the head of a FIFO queue. Sort that vertex's undiscovered neighbours
   by the same pair, mark them discovered and append them to the queue.
4. Repeat until the component ends, then choose the next root. Reverse the
   complete final sequence only if requested.

The sort is per removed vertex, not a global sorting of a whole level. Marking
at enqueue time means an edge to a discovered-but-unprocessed vertex cannot
enqueue it again. Every vertex is removed exactly once. BFS discovers every
vertex reachable from its root, and restarting covers every component, so
the result is a permutation. For each graph edge in a connected component,
its endpoint levels differ by at most one. Placing whole levels consecutively
gives the coarse bound β≤max_j(|V_j|+|V_{j+1}|−1).
Take the maximum over nonempty levels in every component, allowing the next
level to be empty at the end. Small fronts therefore help, but they do not
prove optimal bandwidth.

There is no pseudo-peripheral root search here; a lowest-degree root is a
specific variant. Root and tie choices can differ from other implementations.
The implementation pre-sorts all possible roots once, avoiding a new O(n)
scan for every isolated vertex. Including normalization, sorting and symmetry
checks, time on valid input is O(n log(n+1)+Σ r_i log(r_i+1)), where r_i is
raw row length. Storage is O(n+m_raw). The BFS edge scan alone is linear in
the normalized directed-entry count; calling the entire algorithm O(edges)
would omit sorting, isolated vertices and input preparation.

> Retain the original forty-row experiment

The natural unshuffled pattern connects each of forty ids to its neighbours
at distance one or two. The original seed-seven std::mt19937 shuffle supplies
labels[old]=new. Because the permutation API takes order[new]=old, we pass
its inverse to construct precisely that relabelled graph. The generator,
relabeling, metrics and coordinate extraction all run in the canonical core.
The particular std::shuffle permutation can vary between standard-library
implementations; the reproducible GCC/libstdc++ fixture below has bandwidth
32 before reordering and 2 afterward. The executed guard checks recovery of
the two-wide band, rather than assuming the raw shuffle has identical ids on
every platform.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cuthill_mckee.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <random>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937 rng(7);std::vector<int> labels(40),identity(40);
  std::iota(labels.begin(),labels.end(),0);std::iota(identity.begin(),identity.end(),0);
  std::shuffle(labels.begin(),labels.end(),rng);
  const auto graph=dm::algorithms::permute_symmetric_pattern(dm::algorithms::symmetric_band_pattern(40,2),dm::algorithms::inverse_ordering(labels));
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace(graph,true);
  const auto natural=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,identity);
  const auto forward=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,t.forward_order);
  const auto reverse=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,t.order);
  if(reverse.bandwidth!=2 || forward.bandwidth!=reverse.bandwidth || reverse.edges!=77 || t.adjacency_scans!=154) return 1;
  std::print("| ordering | vertices | edges | bandwidth | lower profile | upper profile |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| original shuffled ids | 40 | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",natural.edges,natural.bandwidth,natural.lower_profile,natural.upper_profile);
  std::print("| CM | 40 | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",forward.edges,forward.bandwidth,forward.lower_profile,forward.upper_profile);
  std::print("| RCM | 40 | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",reverse.edges,reverse.bandwidth,reverse.lower_profile,reverse.upper_profile);
  auto figure=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  for(const auto& order:std::vector<std::vector<int>>{identity,t.order}) {
    auto coordinates=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_coordinates(graph,order);
    for(double& row:coordinates.row) row=-row;
    const auto m=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,order);
    auto panel=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    panel.points(coordinates.column,coordinates.row,"structural entries",{37,99,235},2.5)
         .title(std::format("{}: bandwidth {}",order==identity?"Shuffled ids":"Reverse CM",m.bandwidth))
         .x_label("new column").y_label("negative new row")
         .x_limits(-1,40).y_limits(-40,1).size(520,520);
    figure.add(panel);
  }
  figure.size(1100,560).title("Same 194 structural entries, including forty diagonal positions")
        .save_svg("build/cuthill_mckee.svg");
  std::print("\nActual normalized adjacency scans {}; non-root enqueues {}; components {}.\n",t.adjacency_scans,t.neighbor_enqueues,t.roots.size());
}
```
