| ordering | vertices | edges | bandwidth | lower profile | upper profile |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| original shuffled ids | 40 | 77 | 32 | 557 | 559 |
| CM | 40 | 77 | 2 | 77 | 77 |
| RCM | 40 | 77 | 2 | 77 | 77 |

Actual normalized adjacency scans 154; non-root enqueues 39; components 1.
\image(figures/cuthill_mckee.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Separate panels keep the original and reordered patterns legible. Rows increase downward through negative plotted coordinates. The diagonal is structural; no numerical factorization has been performed.)
\alttext(The left forty by forty pattern is scattered. The right pattern has the same entries in a narrow strip around the diagonal, with half-bandwidth two.)

This successful example recovers the narrow coupling of the constructed
pattern. It is not evidence that CM always shrinks bandwidth, nor a benchmark
of factorization time. For an SPD band matrix, band Cholesky can exploit
O(nβ) storage and O(nβ²) work; actual sparse solvers use symbolic structure,
ordering overhead and hardware-dependent numeric kernels as well.

> Reversal preserves bandwidth; the star separates the objectives

For pʳ[k]=p[n−1−k], its inverse is qʳ[v]=n−1−q[v]. Consequently

\[
|q^r[u]-q^r[v]|=|q[u]-q[v]|,\qquad
\beta(p^r)=\beta(p),\quad L(p^r)=U(p),\quad U(p^r)=L(p).
\]
\alttext(Reversal subtracts each position from n minus one, preserving every edge distance and exchanging lower and upper row-envelope profiles.)

The profile identities follow by exchanging left and right neighbours and
reversing row positions. They hold for any permutation of a symmetric pattern,
not just CM. Lower profile need not fall for an arbitrary reversed ordering.
For this star, CM starts at the smallest leaf, visits the hub, then discovers
the other leaves. CM has bandwidth 3 and lower profile 7; RCM has the same
bandwidth and lower profile 4. A different legal order places the hub centrally
and attains bandwidth 2. Indeed, four distinct neighbours need room within β
positions on either side of the hub, so 2β≥4 proves β≥2; that central order is
optimal. Relabel the same graph by this good order and CM worsens its bandwidth
from 2 to 3. This is an explicit limitation, not just a missing proof.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cuthill_star.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> star{{1,2,3,4},{0},{0},{0},{0}};
  const auto cm=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee(star),rcm=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee(star,true);
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> orders{{1,2,0,3,4},cm,rcm};
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Central hub","CM","Reverse CM"};
  auto figure=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,3);
  std::print("| order | original ids | bandwidth | lower profile | lower skyline slots | symbolic L entries below diagonal |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<orders.size();++k) {
    const auto& order=orders[k];const auto m=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(star,order);
    const auto factor=dm::algorithms::symbolic_cholesky(dm::algorithms::permute_symmetric_pattern(star,order));
    std::size_t entries=0;for(const auto& row:factor) entries+=row.size();
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[k],order,m.bandwidth,m.lower_profile,5+m.lower_profile,entries);
    auto coordinates=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_coordinates(star,order);
    for(double& row:coordinates.row) row=-row;
    auto panel=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    panel.points(coordinates.column,coordinates.row,"",{37,99,235},5)
         .title(std::format("{}: beta {}, L {}",names[k],m.bandwidth,m.lower_profile))
         .x_label("column").y_label("negative row").x_limits(-.5,4.5).y_limits(-4.5,.5).size(360,380);
    figure.add(panel);
  }
  figure.size(1140,440).title("The same star: bandwidth, profile and symbolic fill differ")
        .save_svg("build/cuthill_star.svg");
  const auto good=dm::algorithms::permute_symmetric_pattern(star,orders[0]);
  const auto before=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(good,{0,1,2,3,4});
  const auto after=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(good,dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee(good));
  if(before.bandwidth!=2 || after.bandwidth!=3) return 1;
  std::print("\nCounterexample after central relabeling: input bandwidth {}; CM bandwidth {}.\n",before.bandwidth,after.bandwidth);
}
```
