| order | original ids | bandwidth | lower profile | lower skyline slots | symbolic L entries below diagonal |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Central hub | [1, 2, 0, 3, 4] | 2 | 5 | 10 | 5 |
| CM | [1, 0, 2, 3, 4] | 3 | 7 | 12 | 7 |
| Reverse CM | [4, 3, 2, 0, 1] | 3 | 4 | 9 | 4 |

Counterexample after central relabeling: input bandwidth 2; CM bandwidth 3.
\image(figures/cuthill_star.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The central hub minimizes this star's bandwidth. RCM keeps the CM bandwidth while reducing its lower skyline. The symbolic factor counts come from a separate existing core elimination API; they count below-diagonal structural factor entries, including original edges.)
\alttext(Three five by five sparsity patterns show a central cross, a hub near one end, and its reversed arrangement. Their bandwidths are two, three and three.)

Fill is a third quantity: when a vertex is eliminated, its remaining neighbours
become a clique. That can add edges even within a small band. The symbolic L
counts above exclude the diagonal and include original lower entries; subtract
the four original edges to obtain newly introduced fill. The CM hub is
eliminated with three remaining leaves, creating all three leaf-to-leaf
edges: four original entries plus three fill entries gives seven. The central
hub has two remaining leaves and adds one edge, giving five. RCM removes
three leaves before the hub; its last neighbour creates no pair and no fill,
giving four. A lower skyline is
an envelope allocation bound, not an equality with this count. RCM is useful
for profile-oriented workflows; for fill-focused sparse direct solvers, compare
it with elimination-oriented orderings on the actual problem. No claim of a
universal CM/RCM factorization speed or fill advantage is needed here.

> Count the work, and make invalid inputs observable

For n>w, the width-w band generator has Σ_{d=1}^w(n−d) undirected edges,
so a full traversal scans 2wn−w(w+1) normalized directed entries. The next
experiment counts those actual scans, rather than presenting a noisy elapsed
runtime as a complexity proof. It also checks every returned permutation and
the reversal identities. Fixed w makes scans linear in n; root/neighbour
sorting and normalization still belong in the full cost described above.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cuthill_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <random>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colours{{22,163,74},{37,99,235},{234,88,12}};
  std::mt19937 rng(7);std::size_t series=0;
  std::print("| vertices | generating width | normalized edges | actual directed-entry scans | returned bandwidth |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int w:{1,2,4}) {
    std::vector<double> sizes,scans;
    for(int n:{40,80,160,320,640}) {
      std::vector<int> order(n);std::iota(order.begin(),order.end(),0);std::shuffle(order.begin(),order.end(),rng);
      const auto graph=dm::algorithms::permute_symmetric_pattern(dm::algorithms::symmetric_band_pattern(n,w),order);
      const auto t=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace(graph,true);
      const auto m=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,t.order);
      const auto f=dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics(graph,t.forward_order);
      if(t.adjacency_scans!=static_cast<std::size_t>(2*w*n-w*(w+1)) || t.neighbor_enqueues!=static_cast<std::size_t>(n-1) || m.bandwidth!=f.bandwidth || m.lower_profile!=f.upper_profile || dm::algorithms::inverse_ordering(t.order)!=t.position) return 1;
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,w,m.edges,t.adjacency_scans,m.bandwidth);
      sizes.push_back(n);scans.push_back(t.adjacency_scans);
    }
    plot.line(sizes,scans,std::format("width {}",w),colours[series],2)
        .points(sizes,scans,"",colours[series],4);++series;
  }
  plot.legend({"width 1","width 2","width 4"},colours)
      .title("Actual normalized entry scans, with sorting excluded")
      .x_label("vertices").y_label("directed adjacency entries scanned")
      .x_limits(0,680).y_limits(0,6500).size(900,560).save_svg("build/cuthill_work.svg");
  const auto disconnected=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace({{}, {2}, {1}, {}});
  std::print("\nDisconnected order {}; component roots {}; levels {}.\n",disconnected.order,disconnected.roots,disconnected.level);
  const auto duplicates=dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace({{1,1,0},{0,0,1}});
  std::print("Duplicate/loop normalization: order {}; distinct degrees {}; actual scans {}.\n",duplicates.order,duplicates.degree,duplicates.adjacency_scans);
  int rejections=0;
  try {dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee({{1},{}});} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejections;}
  try {dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace({{1},{0}},false,1,0);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++rejections;}
  try {dm::algorithms::symmetric_pattern_metrics({{1},{0}},{0,0});} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejections;}
  try {dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee_trace({{1},{0},{-1}},false,1,1);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejections;}
  if(rejections!=4 || !dm::algorithms::cuthill_mckee({}).empty()) return 1;
  std::print("Asymmetry, complete vertex cap, duplicate permutation, and late invalid endpoint: {} expected rejections. Empty input returns an empty order.\n",rejections);
}
