\image(figures/cuthill_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual traversal scans follow the exact band-pattern count. This counts one phase of the algorithm, not complete time or allocator cost.)
\alttext(Three straight rising curves show directed adjacency scans against vertex count, with steeper slopes for generating widths one, two and four.)

The trace defaults to at most 100,000 vertices and 1,000,000 raw directed
entries, including ignored loops/duplicates; zero disables the corresponding
optional cap. Complete endpoint and size checks precede these caps and graph
allocation. Reverse-edge symmetry is checked after normalization; an oversized
asymmetric input can therefore reach the size limit first. Caps reject the
whole trace rather than silently truncating its history. The legacy ordering
function retains its original signature and has no optional cap. All owning
results are independent of later input mutation. Invalid ids, asymmetric
patterns and malformed permutations raise invalid_argument; representation
limits, checked count overflow and optional caps raise length_error.

The permanent native tests compare all 33,868 simple labelled graphs through
six vertices against an independent dense/linear-choice BFS reference. They
also inspect every ordering of every simple graph through five vertices
(124,470 cases) against explicit dense matrix positions, including reversal
of profiles. Seeded irregular graphs, row permutations, duplicate/loop
normalization, owned histories, full input checks, coordinate extraction,
pattern round trips and 100,000 isolated vertices cover the surrounding API.
These are algorithmic controls, so an unrelated downloaded dataset would add
no evidence to this example.

> References and reproducibility

- E. Cuthill and J. McKee (1969), “Reducing the bandwidth of sparse symmetric
  matrices,” Proceedings of the 24th ACM National Conference, pp. 157–172:
  https://doi.org/10.1145/800195.805928 . The implementation uses a documented
  deterministic minimum-degree component-start variant.
- W.-H. Liu and A. H. Sherman (1976), “Comparative analysis of the
  Cuthill–McKee and the reverse Cuthill–McKee ordering algorithms for sparse
  matrices,” SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis 13(2), pp. 198–213:
  https://doi.org/10.1137/0713020 .
- SciPy's official reverse_cuthill_mckee interface documents sparse-pattern and
  permutation semantics; its root/tie conventions need not reproduce these ids:
  https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.sparse.csgraph.reverse_cuthill_mckee.html .

Every block is a complete editable C++23 program using the working core via
pkg-config. All SVGs are saved under local build/ and copied into figures/;
result blocks contain tables and file references rather than inline SVG text.
